in this video we will discuss the acute  renal failure now this will be the first  video in two part video series on acute  renal failure in part 1 we will discuss  the etiology definitions types and rifle  criteria of ERF in part ii we will  discuss the clinical features diagnosis  and the treatment of ERF so let's first  get the basics of acute renal failure  clear so here are your kidneys and one  of the main function of the kidneys is  to filter the blood and remove the  nitrogenous waste excess of the fluids  and excess of the electrolytes from the  blood and it is the sudden impairment in  the functioning of the kidney and that  too when happens in a short period of  time it leads to increase in the  concentration of these substances and  there can be a lot of problems that can  arise due to excess of the nitrogenous  waste in the body various type of  electrolyte imbalances in the body and  also fluid overload so let us understand  in brief the consequences of acute  impairment of normal kidney function  first coming to the nitrogenous waste  the nitrogenous waste of the body gets  raised which is represented by increased  concentration of blood urea nitrogen as  well as increased serum creatinine  values various type of electrolyte  imbalances can occur in acute renal  failure like the serum sodium  concentration gets decreased serum  potassium gets raised serum calcium gets  decreased the serum phosphate  concentration is raised when serum pH  gets decreased that is the blood becomes  more acidic one simple trick to remember  all of these electrolyte imbalances is  to remember two sets of ions the sodium  potassium calcium and the phosphate and  to remember that the normal serum sodium  is higher as compared to potassium  whereas the normal serum calcium  concentration is higher as compared to  phosphate and these arrows just get  reversed in case of AR F one important  problem in AR F is the fluid in  balance since kidneys are not  functioning properly the body can have  hypervolemia that is excess body water  or hypovolemia that is deficit body  water hypervolemia mainly occurs in oleg  uric and and uric a RF when the kidneys  do not produce enough urine and the  water gets retained in the body and  hypovolemia occurs in advanced ERF when  the kidneys lose their property of  concentration let us understand how we  define acute renal failure and to define  acute renal failure it is important to  remember the normal values of some renal  function tests like normal serum  creatinine concentration which is 0.8 to  1.3 milligrams per deciliter and normal  urine output which is 1 to 3 milliliters  per kilogram per R and when the serum  creatinine concentration Rises more than  3 milligrams in 48 hours or it rises  more than 1.5 times normal value in less  than 7 days the patient is said to be in  acute renal failure it can also be  defined on basis of urine output when  urine output falls less than 0.5  milliliters per kilogram per hour the  kidneys are said to be in a state of  acute renal failure now let us  understand the types and etiology of  acute renal failure so this is a brief  overview of our urinary system the blood  is filtered to the kidneys the kidneys  produce urine which passes through the  ureters into the bladder urine is then  voided out through the urethra another  important structure which lies here is  the prostate so there is there are three  types of acute renal failure the first  is the pre renal ERF second is the renal  AR F and third is post renal AR F coming  to the original type of AR F it mainly  occurs due to inadequate blood perfusion  to the kidneys kidneys is mainly  supplied by the renal artery and in  situations like hypovolemia when there  is total body water deficit the body is  unable to maintain its proper blood  pressure to maintain a normal perfusion  of kidneys this situation mainly arises  in cases of excessive blood loss  diarrhea and vomiting  burns in which there is fluid loss to  the damaged skin and also pancreatitis  in which there is thirdspace loss  pre-delay RF also occurs due to reduced  cardiac output from the heart which are  which leads to decreased blood pressure  and inadequate perfusion of the kidneys  mainly occurs in cases of heart failure  and also septic shock third important  reason of regional a RF is the diseases  of D renal vessels such as the renal  artery stenosis when the lumen of the  renal artery gets blocked the renal type  of a RF occurs due to diseases which are  primarily localized in the kidney most  of you may know that the kidneys are  mainly made of three types of structures  the first important of them is the  glomeruli and the diseases affecting the  glomeruli are called glomerular  nephritis second important type of  structure in the kidney are the vessels  and various type of vasculitis affect  the vessels and damage them and the  third in point structure of the kidney  are the interstitial and diseases like  acute tubular necrosis effect the  interstitial the post renal type of a RF  occurs due to mechanical obstruction in  the flow of urine and this mainly occurs  due to urinary stones which block the  urinary pathway or also prostatic  enlargement in case of benign prostatic  hyperplasia last section of this video  will deal with the rifle criteria of a  RF the word rifle stands for risk injury  failure loss and end-stage renal disease  all of these five words represent a  grade of acute renal failure in  increasing order of severity in the risk  category the serum creatinine arises 1.5  times of baseline whereas the urine  output of the patient Falls less than  0.5 milliliters per kilogram per hour  for at least six hours in the next  category the injury the serum creatinine  rises two times of baseline whereas the  urine output  Falls 2.5 milliliters per kilogram per  our for at least 12 hours in the next  category the serum creatinine Rises 3  times that of normal whereas the urine  output Falls to 0.3 ml per kilogram per  hour for at least 12 24 hours in the LOS  category of rifle criteria if the acute  kidney injury persists for at least 4  weeks it is included in this category  and end-stage renal disease is when  end-stage renal disease persists for 3  months that is when you require a renal  replacement therapy like dialysis for at  least 3 months thank you so much for  watching this video click the link in  the description below for the past  second of this video subscribe to our  channel on YouTube for all the latest  videos and like our page on Facebook for  all the notifications from met  simplified
 

